Besides the utilization of high-valence metals as hard acids for constructing water stable MOFs, exploiting the azolate ligands (such as imidazolates, pyrazolates, triazolates, and tetrazolates) is another strategy in water stable MOF synthesis.41 As these nitrogen-containing ligands are generally softer ligands, when they interact with the softer divalent metal ions, stronger MOF structures can be formed as a result. The most representative example of this category is the zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), using $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ together with imidizolate linkers to construct a variety of stable crystals analogous to zeolite topology.42–44 Also, Colombo et al. developed the microporous pyrazolate-based MOFs, $\mathbf{M}_{3}\mathbf{(BTP)}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}=N i}$ , Cu, Zn, Co), which exhibited a great hydrothermal stability compared to most carboxylate-based MOFs.45 Following this strategy, there are more and more azolate-based MOFs exhibiting a fair level of hydro-stability developed in recent years, including: MAF series materials (e.g. MAF-6, MAF-7, MAF-49, MAF-X8, etc.) developed by Chen’s group;46–50 and also FIR-54,51 $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{12}(t r z)_{20}][S i W_{12}O_{40}]\cdot11H_{2}O,}^{\xi}}$ 2 $\big[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{trz})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{betc})_{0.5}\big]{\cdot}\mathrm{DM}$ F,53 $\mathrm{Zn_{2}T C S(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}b i p y)}$ ,54 Zn-pbdc-11a(bpe)/- 12a(bpe)/-12a(bpy),55 $\mathrm{Zn(IM)_{1.5}(a b I M)_{0.5},}$ 56 $([\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8})_{0.5^{-}}$ $\left(\mathbf{C_{10}}\mathbf{S_{2}}\mathbf{N_{2}H_{8}}\right)\mathbf{J}{\cdot}5\mathbf{H_{2}O}\right)_{n}$ ,57 $\mathbf{Co/Zn{\mathrm{-}}B T T B B P Y}$ ,58 $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}){\cdot}\right.$ - $\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{3}\big](\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{0.5}$ ,59 $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ ,60 $\mathrm{Cu_{6}(t r z)_{10}(H_{2}O)_{4}[H_{2}S i W_{12}O_{40}]{\cdot}8H_{2}O,^{6}}$ 1 [Ni(BPEB)],62